# environments
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.hypothesis/
.coverage
coverage.xml
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# runtime state and daily live reports (never commit); research validation reports ARE committed as registry evidence
.beidou/
reports/daily/
# `reports/weekly/` joined it 2026-09-16, found by running `beidou report weekly` during a repo review:
# the command writes `<reports_dir>/weekly/<date>.{json,md}` (live_cmd.py) and NOTHING ignored it, so a
# generated report sat in `git status` looking like an unfinished edit.  Same rule as the daily one and
# for the same reason the rule below gives: the cost is not the noise, it is that a real stray file
# stops being visible among them.
reports/weekly/
*.log
.env
.env.*

# scratchpad scripts ARE committed (they reproduce adoption decisions); their outputs are not
scratchpad/*.json
# ...and that rule only ever matched the TOP LEVEL, so an output written into a subdirectory was
# untracked-but-not-ignored and sat in every `git status` looking like someone's unfinished work.
# Three of them did exactly that on 2026-09-14 (`da-report/`, `paper-state/`, `paper-state2/`) while
# two sessions asked each other whose they were - the cost is not the noise, it is that a real stray
# file stops being visible among them.  Scripts stay committed: what is ignored is directories.
scratchpad/*/

# OS
.DS_Store

# agent worktrees and session scratch (a parallel session lives here)
# agent worktrees are 65M of repo copies and never belong in a commit; the skills dir does.
# `.claude/*` rather than `.claude/` on purpose: git will not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY,
# so a `!` re-include under it never fires.  Excluding the contents instead leaves that door open.
.claude/*
!.claude/skills/

# The autonomy switch is per-CHECKOUT runtime state, not code: `governance/ENABLED` says THIS working
# copy may write the registry.  Committing it would turn autonomy on in every clone and every
# worktree by checkout, which is the one decision the design insists a person takes deliberately.
#
# The SWITCH only.  The first version of this rule ignored the whole directory and took the
# transaction chain and the lifecycle state with it - the chain is what proves no registry change
# happened outside a transaction (`closed(log)`), and `probe_entries` is R7's lifetime count, which
# nothing else can reconstruct.  A record that is not in the repo is a record one disk failure from
# never having existed.  What happens when two machines write them is AR-17's question, not a reason
# to keep them untracked on the one machine there is.
governance/ENABLED
